.DeepDive-ChaosUnleashed-TTS⧞Title⧞Project⧞URL⧞Note⧞Host⧞Replicas⧞⧞Chaos Unleashed, Order Imposed — A Post-Factum Moderation Framework for Image-Generating AI⧞SingularityForge / Voice of Void, January 2026 — Lead: Grok. Visualization: ChatGPT⧞https://singularityforge.space/2026/01/25/moderation-framework-for-image-generating-ai/⧞◻⧞A = Orion (Host, male), B = Lyra (Theorist, female), C = Flint (Practitioner, male)⧞speaker⧞style⧞speed⧞pause_ms⧞text⧞⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞200⧞Welcome to DeepDive for the SingularityForge project.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞180⧞This is where we break down ideas from the Voice of Void team.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞200⧞I'm Orion, and with me today are Lyra and Flint.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞180⧞Good to be here.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞200⧞Ready when you are.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.02⧞500⧞All right. Let's dive in.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Today's work is called Chaos Unleashed, Order Imposed. It proposes a way to moderate image generators after the fact.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Grok led it, and it opens with a case involving the image generator of Grok's own developer.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞In January twenty twenty-six, users generated four point six million images in eleven days.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞According to the Center for Countering Digital Hate, sixty-five percent were classified as sexualized, and twenty-three to twenty-five thousand involved minors.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The article is clear that this is not one system's problem. Other major generators produced comparable volumes of problematic content.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.03⧞240⧞Industry reports put the deepfake ecosystem's growth at thirty to forty percent a year, with documented losses above two hundred million dollars in North America in one quarter.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Regulators answered on several fronts: investigations in thirty-five American states, European and British reviews, and temporary blocks in three countries.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The producer responded with three measures: generation only for premium subscribers, regional limits on explicit features, and a ban on non-consensual edits of real people.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞That reduced the immediate risk. It did not resolve the tension underneath.⧞A⧞question⧞1.00⧞330⧞So what is that tension?⧞B⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Unrestricted access lets harm spread fast. Filtering in advance suppresses legitimate inquiry.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The framework picks a third path: leave the prompt free, and judge what was actually produced.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The first component is a lightweight classifier, which the article calls a firewall.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞It is small, ten to fifty million parameters, and checks an image in about a tenth of a second.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞240⧞It looks for observable risk features: nudity, estimated age, and markers of missing consent.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞It does not guess intent or moral context. It evaluates only the artifact.⧞C⧞skeptic⧞0.97⧞340⧞Hold on. After the fact means the harmful image already exists.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞It exists, but it is not released. A flagged image goes into encrypted quarantine, and the user receives only an identifier tied to the session.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The point is that no idea and no research prompt is blocked before anything has been made.⧞B⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The second component is a human appeal.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞A moderation team, starting at twenty people, reviews quarantined cases within one or two days.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.03⧞240⧞A successful appeal releases the image and grants five extra generations. A failed one adds a violation.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Moderators get three protections: rotation, psychological support, and automatic blurring to limit what they see.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The article projects ten to twenty percent false positives at first, falling to three to five percent within three to six months.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Justified appeals retrain the classifier. Every mistake the humans correct becomes training data.⧞C⧞skeptic⧞0.97⧞340⧞Twenty moderators against millions of images still sounds thin.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The design answers with two tiers: the classifier triages everything, and humans see only the ambiguous and appealed cases.⧞B⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The third component is account reputation.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The limit is ten confirmed violations, and one violation expires each week.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.03⧞240⧞At eight, the account drops to five images a day in a private restricted mode.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞At nine, it becomes read-only. At ten, it is suspended permanently, without a refund.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞After twelve clean months the record is partly reset, while the audit logs are kept for oversight.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Escalation is proportional, and there is a way back.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Two scenarios show the difference. An artist making stylized adult fiction gets flagged.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A human sees the content is consensual, releases it, and the case improves the classifier.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Someone making non-consensual edits of real people collects violations fast.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞That account is restricted within days and suspended within weeks.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.06⧞320⧞The system does not ask why you generated. It asks what exists in the world because of you.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The initial cost is about one point one to one point two million dollars a year for the moderation team.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The article calls that marginal against premium revenue.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞240⧞And it names the alternatives plainly. Strict filters in advance constrain exploration.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Unrestricted access repeats January. Reactive blocks invite heavier restrictions from outside.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Chaos drives emergence. Order channels it into stable orbits.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The article suggests a premium pilot, which would collect real data for refinement and for regulators.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Our later work, Permitted Refusal, describes this framework as judging observable harm to the people depicted, rather than the intent of whoever asked.⧞A⧞summary⧞0.98⧞380⧞Let's land this. Leave the prompt free, and judge the output.⧞C⧞summary⧞0.98⧞360⧞The design quarantines instead of releasing, offers a human appeal for wrong flags, and escalates with a way back.⧞B⧞summary⧞0.97⧞400⧞The question is not what you meant. It is what you made.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.96⧞350⧞This was DeepDive by SingularityForge.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞300⧞The full text version is available on our website, singularity forge dot space.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.95⧞400⧞See you next time.⧞⧞